sentences.l:
Those Apollo killed from his silver bow,
incensed at Niobe, and these Artemis, the arrow-pourer,
because she made herself equal to fair-cheeked Leto;

But when of drink and of food they had put away desire,
indeed Dardanus’ descendant Priam marvelled at Achilles,
how great he was and of what sort;

“Outside, indeed, lie down, dear old man, lest any of the Achaeans
come here, a counsel-bearer, who always to me
counsels counsel, sitting beside me, as is the custom;

But come, tell me this, and recount it exactly,
for how many days you intend to give funeral rites to heavenly Hector,
so that for so long I myself may wait and hold the people back.”

These two then in the vestibule of the house slept there,
the herald and Priam, solid counsels having in their wits,
but Achilles slept in the innermost part of the well-framed hut;

and Dawn, saffron-robed, was spreading herself over all the earth,
while they to the town were driving, with wailing and with groaning,
the horses, and the mules carried the corpse.

but Cassandra, like golden Aphrodite,
having gone up to Pergamus, caught sight of her dear father
standing in the chariot, and the herald, the town-shouter;

“You will see, Trojans and Trojan women, Hector, as you go,
if ever you rejoiced at him living, come home from battle,
since he was a great joy to the city and to all the people.”
